    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d85">

                <pb facs="0132" n="122" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg122"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./6–2645: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-26T15:00:00-04:00">June 26, 1945—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>992. Dept informed that Chinese troops attacked Thai troops near Mong Yawng; that
                    Thai withdrew but fear to withdraw further lest arouse Jap suspicions; that Thai
                    have requested SACSEA<anchor n="50" xml:id="d85-f1"/> to ask for Chinese
                    withdrawal; and that US authorities in China have been informed by OSS. Brit and
                    ourselves urged <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ruth</hi><anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d85-f2"/> not to precipitate <pb facs="0133" n="123" xml:id="pg123"
                    /> Jap action against Thai Govt and to delay overt resistance until coordinated
                    with SEAC<anchor n="52" xml:id="d85-f3"/> plans.</p>
                <p>Chinese attacks uncoordinated with SEAC plans might precipitate situation
                    prematurely or else provoke Sino-Thai hostilities. Suggest you discuss with
                    General Wedemeyer the situation, especially its political implications, and keep
                    Dept informed.</p>
                <p>Sent to Chungking. Repeated to London and New Delhi.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d85-f1" n="50" xml:id="d85-f1-content">
                <p>Supreme Allied Commander, Southeast Asia (Mountbatten).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d85-f2" n="51" xml:id="d85-f2-content">
                <p>Code name for the Thai Regent, Pridi Panomyong, head of the Thai underground
                    movement said to have been organized to resist the Japanese and to oppose the
                    Thai Government under Premier Pibul.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d85-f3" n="52" xml:id="d85-f3-content">
                <p>South-East Asia Command.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
